EXT. FROZEN LAKE, YUKON - DAWN

White to every horizon. A snowmobile lies on its side, engine ticking as it cools. Two small figures on the ice.

STRANGER (30s, orange parka, face wrapped in a scarf) crawls from the wreckage, dragging a pack. A woman, COLLEEN MACRAE (50s, ranger's jacket, a bleeding temple) kneels over the broken sled. Both are bundled to the eyes.

STRANGER
Hey! Are you hurt?

COLLEEN
Don't come closer.

STRANGER
I'm just trying to help.

COLLEEN
The ice is cracking. Stay low.

STRANGER
Okay. Okay. I'm down.

She flattens herself, inching forward on elbows. Colleen does the same from the other side.

COLLEEN
What's your name?

STRANGER
Nathalie.

COLLEEN
Nathalie what?

NATHALIE
Okafor-Lindqvist. Nat.

COLLEEN
Nat. You were on the sled when it went?

NAT
I was driving.

COLLEEN
(a pause)
Then it's your fault we're out here.

NAT
Yes, ma'am.

COLLEEN
Don't ma'am me. I'm Colleen.

NAT
Colleen. I can see a cabin. Maybe four hundred yards.

COLLEEN
That's the old trapper's lodge. It's empty. No stove.

NAT
Better than ice.

COLLEEN
Better than nothing.

She reaches toward Colleen. A hairline crack races between them with a sound like a rifle shot. They freeze, hands inches apart.

COLLEEN (CONT'D)
Don't move.

NAT
I'm not.

COLLEEN
Slide your pack. Slowly. Toward me.

Nat pushes it. It slides across the ice, stops beside her knee. She pulls it in and unzips it. Inside: a coil of rope, a flare gun, a thermos. She pulls the rope out and knots a loop.

COLLEEN (CONT'D)
I'll throw this. You tie it around your waist. Then I pull.

NAT
And if the ice breaks?

COLLEEN
Then you'll be glad I tied a good knot.

Colleen throws. Nat catches. She ties it. The crack widens.

NAT
Ready.

COLLEEN
Don't look down. Look at me.

Colleen pulls. Nat slides, ice groaning beneath. Water seeps up in black ribbons. Three feet. Two. One.

Colleen's gloved hand closes around Nat's. They crawl together toward the shore.
